Access to clean water should not be a luxury in Nairobi County. Yet today, thousands of families in our peri-urban and informal settlements still rely on expensive private water vendors or trek long distances to the nearest water point.

Bill Llia today unveiled his **Water for All** plan — a three-year, multi-phase infrastructure program that will bring clean, affordable water to every household in the county.

## The plan

The Water for All initiative has four components:

1. **Borehole rehabilitation** — Over 40% of the county's boreholes are non-functional due to poor maintenance. The plan will rehabilitate all county-owned boreholes within the first year and establish a dedicated maintenance unit to keep them running.

2. **Pipeline expansion** — Extend the water distribution network to reach underserved wards, prioritizing areas that currently rely on water trucking. The plan targets 200 kilometers of new pipeline in the first two years.

3. **Community water kiosks** — Install 500 community water kiosks in partnership with community-based organizations, ensuring affordable water (at or below KES 5 per 20-liter jerrycan) within 500 meters of every household.

4. **Rainwater harvesting** — Provide subsidies and technical assistance for households and schools to install rainwater harvesting systems, reducing dependence on the main grid and building climate resilience.

## What it will cost

The total investment required is estimated at KES 4.5 billion over three years, to be funded through a combination of county budget allocation, national government infrastructure grants, and development partner financing.

"Every shilling spent on water infrastructure is an investment in health, dignity, and economic productivity," Bill said. "When a mother doesn't have to spend two hours fetching water, she can spend that time working, learning, or caring for her children. That is the return on investment we are after."

## Community voices

At the announcement in Mathare, resident Grace Wanjiku shared her story: "I wake up at 4 AM every day to queue for water. Some days the vendor doesn't come, and we have to buy from private sellers who charge five times the price. I want my children to grow up in a Nairobi where they turn on the tap and water comes out."

"Grace's story is the story of millions of Nairobi residents," Bill responded. "And it is a story we will change — together."
